keanu reeves career stretches right back  into the 1980s  and well let's just say he's had his  fair share of ups and downs  from killer action flicks and  heartwarming dramas to tacky thrillers  and cheap sci-fi  these are the best and worst keanu  reeves movies  everyone knows that a dog is a man's  best friend so when a group of russian  gangsters break into john wick's home  kill his puppy and drive off in his car  he does what any notorious assassin  would do he makes it his mission to hunt  these men down and destroy them  but make no mistake john wick is more  than just a montage of mindless fight  scenes  the cinematography is striking the  pacing will keep you hooked and the  world building is genuinely intriguing  on top of that reeves performance as a  man haunted by grief and loss is  absolutely top notch and it doesn't  exactly hurt that he nails the action  sequences either  so far the original film has spawned two  sequels john wick chapter 2 and john  wick chapter 3 parabellum  both movies have earned positive  receptions from critics and audiences  and the story is even set to continue in  may 2022 with another installment john  wake chapter 4.  yeah i'm thinking i'm back  and for the record every single film on  the franchise so far really has been  amazing so if you want to see reeves as  action star best  this is the place to start in the iffy  romantic crime thriller siberia reeve  stars is lucas hill  an american diamond merchant who lands  himself in trouble after traveling to  russia to sell a rare variety of blue  diamonds to the gangster boris volkov  it kinda seems like lucas should have  anticipated that this transaction  wouldn't work out in his favor  and sure enough when he arrives he finds  that his contact with the diamonds has  disappeared  now lucas is desperate to track him down  before boris comes looking for what he's  owed  you'd be forgiven for assuming that a  film involving a diamond heist and a  forbidden romance would bring a little  more excitement to the screen than  siberia does  but sadly this movie's plot is kind of  plotting and dull but what makes siberia  so frustrating is how seriously this  film takes itself  you can't even look at it as the kind of  movie that's so bad that it's actually  good  much like an actual trek through siberia  the entire journey is a long slog from  beginning to end  and you wouldn't be blamed for wanting  to give up halfway through  one of the all-time classic comedies  bill and ted's excellent adventure  follows the long strange journey of high  school slackers bill s  preston and theodore ted logan their  high school slackers struggling to pass  their upcoming history test  and even worse ted's father is making  plans to ship him off to military  school if he can't earn a decent grade  of course doing that would alter the  course of the future  as these two are destined to save all of  humanity with their rock and roll music  so rufus a citizen of the future travels  back to the past to help them ace their  test  as a result the heroic duo ends up going  on a time traveling adventure enlisting  familiar historical figures to help them  pass their test  it's a fantastic premise and even though  it's one of his earliest films reeves is  absolutely fantastic proving he's a more  than capable comedy  actor of course if you need more bill  and ted in your life never fear  excellent adventure inspired two more  sequels bogus journey and base the music  and who doesn't need a little more bill  and ted in their lives  excellent  and exposed reeves plays detective scott  galbin a man who's trying to uncover the  mystery behind the shocking death of his  corrupt partner detective joey cullen  however cullen's colleagues worry that  digging into his past will result in bad  pr for the police department  things get even stranger when galvin  realizes that a young woman named isabel  with seemingly no prior connection to  cullen might be involved with the case  somehow  unfortunately exposed was pretty much  doomed from the get-go  the film was originally titled daughter  of god and the plot was supposed to  focus predominantly on isabelle and her  family but the studio behind the movie  decided that centering the storyline  around reeves character would result in  higher returns at the box office  the film went through a number of  substantial changes in post-production  resulting in a very different movie than  the one the director had initially  planned  it's clear that this was a misstep in  the end and it's a real shame that the  original narrative never saw the light  of day  my own private idaho stands out from the  rest of risa's earlier work in hollywood  it's loosely based on a series of  shakespeare plays but director gus van  sant adapted the bard's classic tale  into a thoroughly modern story  even now it's regarded as a classic  example of new queer cinema  and while the subject matter wouldn't be  viewed as particularly taboo today  it definitely pushed boundaries back in  the early 1990s the film was also  notable for its creative avant-garde  visual style  as for the plot reeves and his real-life  friend river phoenix co-stars the  wandering protagonist scott  mike these two friends work as street  hustlers in portland but when mike  decides that he desperately needs to  find his mother they set off on a  journey that takes him all the way to  italy  mike finds himself falling for scott and  the two have to navigate the intense  emotions tied up in their complicated  relationship  as a result the film is very different  from reeves more action-heavy movies but  it proved to a skeptical world that the  actor really did have some serious  dramatic chops  replicas was universally panned by  critics and it wasn't much of a hit with  audiences either to be fair to reeves  though he was probably the only bright  spot in this practically unwatchable  film  but try as he might his performance just  couldn't salvage this truly dire movie  reeves plays william foster a  neuroscientist who ventures into some  pretty unethical territory while caught  in the throes of grief  after his wife and children die in a car  crash she decides to try cloning them  he knows he has to keep this experiment  a secret but he can't hide it forever  and naturally this risky decision comes  with some unintended consequences  admittedly the idea behind replicas is  actually quite interesting and with  better execution this could have been a  far more engaging film  but with numerous plot holes and a cast  of characters who just can't seem to  stop themselves from making the worst  possible decision in every scenario  watching replicas becomes little more  than an exercise in frustration  speed was one of reeves first major  blockbuster films and was arguably the  movie that catapulted him to superstar  status  reeve stars is jack traven a swat  officer working with the lapd  jack and his partner think they've  managed to stop extortionist bomber  howard payne after he attempted to hold  a group of elevator passengers for  ransom  as it turns out however payne is also  rigged a bus with a bomb  and if the vehicle's speed drops below  50 miles per hour it will explode and  kill  everyone on board now it's up to jack to  defuse the explosive and get everyone to  safety  admittedly the premise of speed comes  across as kind of silly but as the movie  picks up it's nearly impossible to look  away from the screen  the genuinely sizzling on-screen  chemistry between reeves and his co-star  sandra bullock certainly didn't hurt  either  and then of course there's the bus jump  one of hollywood's best ever driving  stunts  and it was totally real too  all in all this is reeves at his 90s  best just don't mention the sequel  based on a william gibson story of the  same name johnny mnemonic sees reeve  star as its titular character  the plot is clever enough johnny has a  cybernetic brain  implant that can store all kinds of  information and in a dystopian society  ruled by megacorporations where everyone  is forced to be connected to the  internet his abilities come in very  handy indeed  in johnny's world transmitting sensitive  data can be dangerous  so as a mnemonic courier it's up to him  to carry certain information to  different parties  however doing so means risking losing  his childhood memories  and he wants to remove his implant to  get them back unfortunately for johnny  however his work isn't quite done yet  johnny mnemonic didn't exactly get a  glowing response from the critics  in fact reeves actually earned himself a  razzy nomination for worst actor so it's  safe to say that this role was  a new low point in his career although  it was marketed as an exciting  cyberpunk thriller the plot falls  totally flat and worse still it is aged  very badly indeed  even for a 90s film sadly there's just  nothing all that memorable about the  futuristic  world of johnny mnemonic the matrix was  more than just a film  it was a cultural phenomenon the movie  won four academy awards and left a  lasting impact on an entire generation  of filmmakers  and while reeves was already a  well-known actor when the matrix was  released  this film brought his career to a whole  new level in this masterpiece from the  wachowski sisters reeve stars as neo a  hacker who's unknowingly living in a  dystopia where humanity has been trapped  in a simulated reality called the matrix  this simulation was designed by machines  so they could control human beings by  keeping them in pods all while  harvesting their bodies for energy  when neo discovers the truth about his  world he's given a choice  he can take the blue pill and return to  his old life or he can take the red pill  and finally accept reality  and even though he knows he can never  truly go back neo nonetheless steps up  to lead the rebellion against the  machines  the resulting movie is full to the brim  with philosophical musings incredible  martial arts fights and some of the most  recognizable and iconic quotes in  cinematic history  i know kung fu  show me the matrix also gave audiences a  truly classic villain and agent smith  gifted film fans with one of the coolest  opening scenes ever plus one of cinema's  craziest gun fights  the movie revolutionized hollywood with  its innovative camera work and brought  newfound fame and respect to reeves  himself who delivered a fascinating  zen-like performance  honestly the matrix isn't just one of  keanu reeves greatest films it's one of  the greatest science fiction films of  all time  generation um however might just be the  worst out of reeves entire career  even the title is underwhelming and  things don't exactly improve from there  this mumble core indie flick was so  unwatchable that it actually managed to  score a whopping zero percent on rotten  tomatoes  in generation um reeves plays john an  escort service driver who's lost all  sense of direction in life the film  explores 24 hours of his aimless  existence in new york city in which he  meets up with two young women violet and  mia  this is an attempt to delve into the  concept of urban alienation but the  movie falls far  short of its attention in one scene for  example jon points a camcorder at the  women and asks them to say something  interesting  neither offers up anything particularly  poignant and that basically sums up the  film as a whole  in the end this movie is basically a  shrug of the shoulders  and it quickly comes to the tired  conclusion that young people these days  just might be a little vain and  self-absorbed  not exactly revolutionary stuff is it  check out one of our newest videos right  here plus even more looper videos about  your favorite movies are coming soon  subscribe to our youtube channel and hit  the bell so you don't miss a single one
 

